WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.088
Blue, the offset to a query point. Yellow, the particle's state.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.769
Take the offset's length. Split the state: size and direction.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.081
Their angle gives a cosine: here, one half.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:18.194
No rotation changes these. The scalar branch makes sixty-four positive numbers.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.901
The vector branch makes sixty-four small arrows.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.742
Each number, times a learned weight, scales its arrow.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.286
Added tip to tail, they make the message.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.611
It nearly matches exact Stokes flow.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.256
The message lies in the inputs' plane, so two coefficients, A and B, rebuild it.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.668
Turn the inputs, and the message turns too.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.291
Green check: the numbers changed only by rounding error.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.076
A tilt or a mirror does the same: numbers stay, arrows turn.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:00.003
The message is invariant numbers times the input vectors: A, times the offset, plus B, times the direction.
